# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPO-WIDE BASELINE. Everything below this block is a more specific rule and
# still wins (.gitattributes is last-match-wins); the specific rules are kept
# because they document WHY each file individually cannot tolerate a CR.
#
# Why a baseline exists at all. This product's claim is that a stranger can
# audit the source that produced the artifact. That claim requires the bytes
# Inno bundles to BE the bytes in the repo, and until v1.4.3 they were not:
# `git ls-files --eol` found 118 tracked files whose working-tree form differed
# from their index form, TEN of them in the .iss [Files] section. `git status`
# was clean the whole time, because a `text` attribute makes git normalise on
# COMPARISON -- so a stale CRLF working copy compares equal to an LF index for
# ever, and git never re-normalises an existing working copy when a rule is
# added later. That is how six files carrying `text eol=lf` below were still
# CRLF on disk.
#
# Without this baseline the checked-out form of every UNPINNED file is decided
# by core.autocrlf, which on this build machine is `true` and comes from the
# SYSTEM git config -- i.e. from the Git for Windows installer, not from
# anything versioned here. A fresh clone on a differently configured machine
# produced different artifact bytes, silently. Pinning it in the repo is the
# only form of the fix that travels with the repo.
#
# text=auto (not a bare `text`) so git still auto-detects binaries and leaves
# ClawChat.exe, the .ico and the .png alone. Verified: no tracked file has a
# CRLF index form, so this changes no committed blob -- only the checkout.
#
# scripts/build_release.ps1 enforces this for the bundled set as a build gate
# ("Worktree pin"), so a future divergence fails the build instead of shipping.
* text=auto eol=lf
# Scripts transported into WSL and executed by bash/node MUST stay LF.
# A CR in the shebang or `set -e` line breaks them ("bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M").
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise give CRLF working copies on Windows.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-spend-check.js text eol=lf
# v1 Guard 1. clawfactory-quarantine-rm.js is installed as the agent's `rm` and is
# executed through its SHEBANG, so a trailing CR there is fatal ("bad interpreter"),
# not merely untidy. install-quarantine.sh CR-strips at runtime as well.
resources/quarantine-lib.js            text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-quarantined.js   text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-quarantinectl.js text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-quarantine-rm.js text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-quarantine.service    text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-quarantine-gc.service text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-quarantine-gc.timer   text eol=lf
# v1 Guard 2. clawfactory-send.js is installed as /usr/local/bin/clawfactory-send and
# clawfactory-sendd.js / clawfactory-sendctl.js run from their shebangs, so a trailing
# CR is fatal rather than untidy. install-send.sh CR-strips at runtime as well.
resources/send-lib.js                  text eol=lf
resources/send-smtp.js                 text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-sendd.js         text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-sendctl.js       text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-send.js          text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-send.service     text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-send-gc.service  text eol=lf
resources/clawfactory-send-gc.timer    text eol=lf
resources/egress-policy.json           text eol=lf

# v1.1.0 (JOB 3B): structural defense-in-depth so no future multi-line bash carried
# into WSL from a .ps1 (e.g. a probe's Wsl helper, the adversarial suite) can regress
# on CRLF ("unexpected end of file"). The runtime CR-strip in those helpers stays as
# belt-and-suspenders; this makes LF the stored + checked-out form. See F2 L20/L21.
*.ps1  text eol=lf
# EXCEPTION: setup.ps1 is stored byte-for-byte, never EOL-renormalized. It used to
# carry `binary`, which is shorthand for `-text -diff`: the `-text` half is the part
# that protects the bytes, and the `-diff` half was collateral. The cost of that
# collateral was total: `git diff` reported only "Binary files differ" and `grep`
# refused the file, so every change to the most security-critical file we ship went
# in unreviewable. `-text diff` keeps the byte protection and gives the diff back.
#
# The literal NUL byte that originally triggered git's binary auto-detection (it sat
# inside a comment ABOUT NUL bytes) has been removed. It was in a `#` comment, so
# removing it cannot change behaviour, and with it gone nothing auto-detects either.
setup.ps1 -text diff

# v1 persona-as-a-constant. The frozen workspace SOUL is composed from these two
# files, and its SHA-256 is baked into setup.ps1 at build time, so their bytes
# must be identical in every clone. Without an explicit rule these follow
# core.autocrlf, which means a clone on a differently configured machine would
# produce a different digest and the install would refuse its own safety rules.
# safety-rules.md was CRLF on disk until this rule landed; normalising it changes
# $expectedSoulHash, which is why that literal was recomputed in the same commit.
resources/safety-rules.md text eol=lf
resources/persona.md      text eol=lf

# v1 Guard 3. clawfactory-fetchctl.js runs from its shebang inside WSL, so a
# trailing CR is fatal rather than untidy ("bad interpreter"). setup.ps1 also
# normalizes line endings when it transports the file, and this makes LF the
# stored and checked-out form so the two cannot disagree. Same class as L20/L21.
resources/clawfactory-fetchctl.js text eol=lf

# The released-artifact ledger is PARSED by scripts/build_release.ps1 (split on
# tab, one row per shipped artifact) and appended to with an explicit LF. Pinning
# it means a clone on a differently configured machine reads the same rows rather
# than picking up stray CRs in the digest field and failing to match a version it
# has genuinely already shipped.
released-versions.tsv text eol=lf

# The licence text is digest-checkable against the canonical Apache-2.0 file
# (sha256 cfc7749b96f63bd31c3c42b5c471bf756814053e847c10f3eb003417bc523d30).
# A CRLF checkout would break that match, so pin both files to LF.
LICENSE text eol=lf
NOTICE  text eol=lf
